{
  "id": "nobody-answers-a-private-address",
  "name": "Nobody Answers a Private Address",
  "category": "security",
  "difficulty": 4,
  "xp": 350,
  "level": 10,
  "description": "Traffic reaches the internet and never comes back.",
  "brief": "PC-01 sits in 192.168.1.0/24 — a private range that exists on countless networks at once, which is exactly why nothing on the wider internet knows how to route to it.\n\nWatch what happens: the request reaches SITE-01 quite happily. The reply is addressed to 192.168.1.10, and ISP-01 has never heard of that network, so it drops it.\n\nRouter-01 owns a real public address. Make the traffic leave wearing it.",
  "hints": [
    "Test PC-01 → 198.51.100.10 on port 80 and read where it stopped.",
    "Which address is ISP-01 complaining that it has no route to?",
    "Router-01 has a public address on the interface facing ISP-01.",
    "Turn on NAT on Router-01 and pick that interface as the public one."
  ],
  "explanation": "Private addresses are not routable on the public internet — every network is free to use them, so they cannot be unique. The request arrived only because the reply had not been needed yet; the moment the server answered, the private source address became the problem.\n\nNAT solves it by rewriting the source to the router's own public address and remembering the swap, so the reply comes back to something the internet can actually route to and the router can put it back where it belongs. 'show ip nat translations' shows those bindings.",
